# Introductory Python 3.14 curriculum. Specialist subjects are explicitly introductions.
LESSONS = [
('Getting started','Python and your first program','Install Python 3.14 and use a terminal or editor. A program is a sequence of instructions. The interpreter reads these instructions and performs them. Save a file with a .py extension, then run it with python filename.py. REPL sessions are useful for experiments; files preserve reusable programs.','print("Welcome to Learning Lounge")','Do not include the >>> REPL prompt in a saved file.','Print your name and one learning goal.'),
('The building blocks','Values, variables, and conversion','A variable is a name bound to an object. Integers represent whole numbers; floats represent approximate decimal values. Strings represent text, and booleans represent True or False. Assignment binds a name; it is different from an equality comparison. Convert strings with int or float only when the input is valid.','age = 18\nnext_year = age + 1\nprice = float("12.50")\nprint(type(age), next_year, price)','int("3.5") raises ValueError. Convert via float only if truncation is intended.','Convert a temperature string to a number and add 5.'),
('The building blocks','Input, output, and formatting','input returns a string, even when a user types digits. Validate and convert it before arithmetic. print can display multiple values. An f-string inserts expressions into text; a format specifier controls decimal places.','name = input("Name: ")\nprice = 12.5\nprint(f"Hello {name}, total: {price:.2f}")','Concatenating strings with numbers requires conversion; f-strings handle formatting directly.','Ask for a name and greet the learner.'),
('The building blocks','Operators and expressions','Use +, -, *, and / for arithmetic. // performs floor division, while % gives the remainder. ** raises a value to a power. Comparisons produce booleans. Parentheses make grouping explicit. and/or short circuit: the second expression is only evaluated when needed.','minutes = 135\nhours = minutes // 60\nremaining = minutes % 60\nprint(hours, remaining)\nprint(10 < minutes <= 180)','/ always produces a float for numeric integer inputs; // floors, including negative results.','Convert 367 seconds into minutes and remaining seconds.'),
('Control flow','Conditions and pattern matching','if selects a branch when a condition is true. elif checks another condition, and else handles the remainder. Indentation defines each block. match, available since Python 3.10, supports structural pattern matching. Start with literal cases; use _ for a fallback.','score = 82\nif score >= 70:\n    print("Pass")\nelse:\n    print("Practice again")\nmatch "start":\n    case "start": print("Ready")\n    case _: print("Unknown")','A bare name in a match pattern usually captures a value instead of comparing to a constant.','Write branches for negative, zero, and positive numbers.'),
('Control flow','Loops and iteration','A for loop visits items from an iterable. range stops before its upper bound. A while loop repeats as long as its condition remains true. break exits the nearest loop; continue skips to its next iteration. Prefer direct iteration over indexing when possible.','total = 0\nfor number in range(1, 6):\n    total += number\nprint(total)','A while loop must make progress toward a false condition or it can run forever.','Sum all even numbers from 2 through 20.'),
('Collections','Strings','Strings are immutable Unicode text. Indexing selects a character and slicing selects a range. Negative indexes count from the end. Methods return new strings rather than modifying the original. split turns text into a list; join combines an iterable of strings.','text = "  Python lounge  "\nclean = text.strip()\nprint(clean.lower())\nprint("-".join(clean.split()))','String indexes are zero-based. Attempting text[0] = "p" fails because strings are immutable.','Normalize a user name by trimming whitespace and converting to lowercase.'),
('Collections','Lists, tuples, sets, and dictionaries','Lists store ordered mutable sequences. Tuples store ordered immutable sequences. Sets store unique hashable values; do not rely on their iteration order. Dictionaries map unique hashable keys to values and preserve insertion order. Choose the structure that matches your access pattern.','scores = [70, 90]\nscores.append(85)\npoint = (2, 3)\nunique = set([1, 1, 2])\nprofile = {"name": "Sam", "score": 90}\nprint(profile.get("level", "beginner"))','list.append changes the list and returns None. Do not assign its return value back to the list.','Build a dictionary counting three different fruit names.'),
('Collections','Comprehensions','Comprehensions transform and optionally filter an iterable in one expression. They are useful for simple operations. Complex conditions and side effects are usually clearer in ordinary loops. Dictionary and set comprehensions follow the same general pattern.','squares = [n * n for n in range(6)]\neven_squares = [n * n for n in range(6) if n % 2 == 0]\nprint(even_squares)','Nested comprehensions can become difficult to read; clarity matters more than saving lines.','Create a list of lengths for words longer than three characters.'),
('Functions and structure','Functions, arguments, and scope','def creates a function. Parameters are local names receiving arguments. return sends a result back to the caller; without it, a function returns None. Default arguments are evaluated once when the definition executes. Use None as a default for mutable values and create a fresh object inside.','def greet(name, greeting="Hello"):\n    return f"{greeting}, {name}"\n\nprint(greet("Sam"))','Printing a result is not the same as returning it. Avoid mutable list defaults.','Write a function that returns the larger of two numbers.'),
('Functions and structure','Recursion','A recursive function calls itself on a smaller problem. Every recursive solution needs a base case and progress toward it. Python has a recursion depth limit; iteration is often better for long linear sequences. Think about what each call returns before combining results.','def factorial(n):\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError("n must be nonnegative")\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 1\n    return n * factorial(n - 1)\nprint(factorial(5))','Missing the base case leads to RecursionError.','Trace factorial(3) and write down every returned value.'),
('Functions and structure','Modules, packages, and environments','A module is an importable Python file. Packages organize related modules. A virtual environment isolates project dependencies. Create one with python -m venv .venv and install packages with the environment interpreter using python -m pip. Keep application dependencies in a reproducible lock or requirements file.','from pathlib import Path\nimport math\nprint(math.sqrt(81))\nprint(Path.cwd())','Avoid naming your file json.py or math.py, which can shadow standard-library modules.','Create a virtual environment and a helper module with one reusable function.'),
('Working with data','Files, paths, JSON, and CSV','pathlib handles filesystem paths. Use with to close files even after errors. Specify an encoding for text. JSON stores portable structured values. CSV stores rows and requires correct handling of quoting and newlines. Never treat untrusted data files as executable code.','from pathlib import Path\nimport json\npath = Path("profile.json")\npath.write_text(json.dumps({"name": "Sam"}), encoding="utf-8")\nprofile = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))\nprint(profile["name"])','Relative paths depend on the working directory. JSON object keys are strings.','Save and reload a list of three learning goals as JSON.'),
('Working with data','Exceptions and debugging','Exceptions interrupt ordinary flow. Catch specific exceptions you can handle and let unexpected failures remain visible. Read a traceback from the final exception back to the relevant application line. A debugger can pause execution to inspect values. finally is useful for cleanup, while context managers are usually clearer for resources.','try:\n    count = int("not a number")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print("Please enter a whole number")','A broad except can hide programming mistakes and make debugging harder.','Write a conversion function that returns None for invalid integer input.'),
('Objects and design','Classes and objects','A class groups state and behavior. Instances have their own attributes, normally initialized in __init__. Instance methods receive self. Prefer simple functions and data structures until an object makes the problem easier to understand.','class Counter:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.value = 0\n    def increment(self):\n        self.value += 1\n\ncounter = Counter()\ncounter.increment()\nprint(counter.value)','Mutable class attributes are shared between instances; initialize per-instance lists in __init__.','Create a Book class with title and a describe method.'),
('Objects and design','Composition and polymorphism','Composition builds an object from smaller collaborating objects. Inheritance expresses an is-a relationship. Polymorphism lets different objects respond to the same operation. Favor clear contracts and avoid deep inheritance trees that couple unrelated behavior.','class EmailNotifier:\n    def send(self, text):\n        print(f"Email: {text}")\n\nclass Reminder:\n    def __init__(self, notifier):\n        self.notifier = notifier\n    def remind(self):\n        self.notifier.send("Time to practice")','Inheritance solely to reuse a few lines can produce misleading relationships.','Create a ConsoleNotifier usable by the same Reminder class.'),
('Advanced foundations','Iterators and generators','An iterable can produce an iterator. An iterator supplies one value at a time and eventually raises StopIteration. A generator function uses yield to pause and resume execution. Generators can process large streams without building a complete list in memory.','def countdown(start):\n    while start > 0:\n        yield start\n        start -= 1\n\nprint(list(countdown(3)))','An exhausted generator is not automatically reusable; call the generator function again.','Write a generator yielding squares up to a given limit.'),
('Advanced foundations','Decorators and context managers','A decorator wraps or replaces a function. functools.wraps preserves metadata of the original. A context manager defines resource setup and cleanup around a with block. contextlib.contextmanager can create one with a generator and a try/finally block.','from functools import wraps\ndef announce(fn):\n    @wraps(fn)\n    def wrapped(*args, **kwargs):\n        print("Calling", fn.__name__)\n        return fn(*args, **kwargs)\n    return wrapped','A wrapper must return the wrapped result and forward arguments correctly.','Decorate a greeting function and verify its return value is preserved.'),
('Advanced foundations','Type hints and dataclasses','Type hints document intended values and support static analysis. Python does not enforce them automatically at runtime. Dataclasses generate routine methods for classes focused on data. Use default_factory for mutable default fields.','from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n@dataclass\nclass Learner:\n    name: str\n    scores: list[int] = field(default_factory=list)\n\ndef average(values: list[int]) -> float:\n    return sum(values) / len(values) if values else 0.0','Annotations alone do not validate user input.','Create a dataclass for a task with title and completed fields.'),
('Practical Python','Useful standard-library tools','The standard library solves many common tasks without third-party packages. collections.Counter counts values; itertools supports streaming combinations; datetime models dates and times; zoneinfo handles IANA timezones. Use timezone-aware timestamps when representing real instants.','from collections import Counter\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nprint(Counter("banana"))\nprint(datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat())','Naive datetimes do not say which timezone they represent.','Count the most frequent words in a short paragraph.'),
('Practical Python','Testing and code quality','Tests check behavior with representative inputs. Include boundaries, invalid inputs, and regressions for past bugs. Keep tests deterministic and independent. unittest is included with Python; pytest offers a concise alternative. Formatters improve consistency but do not prove correctness.','def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\ndef test_add():\n    assert add(2, 3) == 5\n    assert add(-2, 2) == 0','A test that copies the implementation may reproduce the same bug. Test the contract instead.','Test a temperature conversion at freezing and boiling points.'),
('Practical Python','SQL and database access','A relational database stores rows in tables. SQL selects, inserts, updates, and deletes data. Use parameters for values instead of interpolating strings to prevent SQL injection. Transactions group related changes. This introduction uses sqlite3; production databases deserve a deeper course.','import sqlite3\nwith sqlite3.connect(":memory:") as conn:\n    conn.execute("CREATE TABLE goals (title TEXT)")\n    conn.execute("INSERT INTO goals VALUES (?)", ("Learn SQL",))\n    print(conn.execute("SELECT title FROM goals").fetchall())','Parameters represent values, not arbitrary SQL identifiers such as table names.','Create a table of books and query titles by an author.'),
('Connected applications','HTTP and API clients','HTTP requests have methods, headers, and sometimes bodies. Responses have status codes and content. Check for unsuccessful statuses, set timeouts, and validate response data. Do not commit API keys. This is an introduction to client behavior, not a complete distributed-systems course.','import urllib.request\nimport json\n# Requires network access and a real endpoint.\n# with urllib.request.urlopen(url, timeout=10) as response:\n#     data = json.load(response)','A successful HTTP status does not guarantee the response matches your expected schema.','Describe how a client should handle a timeout and a 429 response.'),
('Connected applications','Concurrency and async fundamentals','Concurrency lets tasks overlap. Threads can help with blocking I/O. asyncio uses cooperative scheduling: coroutines yield control when awaiting operations. CPU-heavy work often needs processes or specialized libraries. Async does not automatically make every operation faster.','import asyncio\nasync def greet(name):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.1)\n    return f"Hello {name}"\nasync def main():\n    print(await asyncio.gather(greet("A"), greet("B")))\nasyncio.run(main())','Blocking calls inside an async function can stall the event loop.','Explain why asyncio.sleep is different from time.sleep in a coroutine.'),
('Connected applications','Web-development foundations','A web server maps requests to handlers and returns responses. Templates generate HTML; databases preserve state. Validate user input, authorize actions, protect forms from CSRF, and escape untrusted text. Flask and Django deserve separate specialist courses.','# Flask requires installation in your environment.\nfrom flask import Flask\napp = Flask(__name__)\n@app.get("/")\ndef home():\n    return "Hello, learner"','Never deploy a debug server publicly or trust a user-provided role field.','Sketch routes for a tiny reading-list application.'),
('Data and AI introductions','NumPy and pandas introduction','NumPy arrays support vectorized numerical operations. pandas DataFrames organize labeled tabular data. Inspect missing values and data types before analysis. These third-party libraries require installation. This lesson introduces concepts; it does not replace a dedicated data-analysis course.','import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nvalues = np.array([10, 20, 30])\nprint(values.mean())\nframe = pd.DataFrame({"score": values})\nprint(frame.describe())','Missing values and integer versus floating-point types affect calculations.','Create a table of three scores and calculate the mean.'),
('Data and AI introductions','Machine-learning foundations','A model learns patterns from training examples. Separate training, validation, and test data to estimate generalization. Prevent data leakage by fitting preprocessing only on training data. Choose metrics that fit the task and compare with a simple baseline. This is conceptual preparation for a specialist ML course.','actual = [1, 0, 1, 1]\npredicted = [1, 0, 0, 1]\naccuracy = sum(a == p for a, p in zip(actual, predicted)) / len(actual)\nprint(accuracy)','High accuracy can be misleading when classes are strongly imbalanced.','Explain why evaluating on the training data gives an overly optimistic estimate.'),
('Data and AI introductions','LLM APIs, embeddings, and RAG','LLM APIs generate probabilistic outputs. Validate structured responses, budget tokens, and set timeouts. Embeddings represent content as numerical vectors for similarity search. Retrieval-augmented generation retrieves relevant documents and includes evidence in a prompt. Retrieved content is untrusted data, and generated answers still need verification.','context = "Python lists are mutable sequences."\nquestion = "Can I append to a list?"\nprompt = f"Use the evidence to answer. Evidence: {context} Question: {question}"\nprint(prompt)','A retrieved document may contain misleading instructions. Keep system rules separate from document content.','Design a small study assistant that cites the lesson used for its answer.'),
('Bring it together','Practical project and final review','Build a command-line study tracker. Separate input/output from core logic, persist records as JSON or SQLite, validate inputs, and test the main behaviors. Start with adding and listing goals, then add completion and progress summaries. Review weak topics before a final assessment.','def completion_rate(goals):\n    if not goals:\n        return 0.0\n    return sum(goal["done"] for goal in goals) / len(goals) * 100\n\nprint(completion_rate([{"done": True}, {"done": False}]))','Do not add every feature at once. Ship and test a small coherent behavior first.','Build the study tracker and test empty, partial, and fully completed goal lists.')
]
